WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.640
Most actors will eventually find themselves&nbsp;
locking lips with another actor who, in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.840
has nothing to do with them romantically.&nbsp;
Sometimes, these kisses have little to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:18.320
no consequences. But other times, onscreen&nbsp;
kisses can get actors in trouble in real life.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.320
In 1995, Star Trek: Deep Space&nbsp;
Nine aired what proved to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.480
one of the most controversial&nbsp;
episodes of the entire franchise.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.880
Jadzia Dax takes center stage in season 4's&nbsp;
"Rejoined," where she's reunited with Lenara Kahn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.120
Dax and Lenara are "joined Trill," who share&nbsp;
their existence with slug-like symbionts that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.320
carry their memories and experiences from&nbsp;
host to host over the course of centuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.160
With their respective previous hosts, Dax&nbsp;
and Lenara were once married. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.760
it's forbidden for "joined Trill" to become&nbsp;
involved with romantic partners from previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.960
lifetimes. So, when they kiss,&nbsp;
it definitely has consequences.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.000
This happened to be the case in the&nbsp;
real world as well. Dax actress Terry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.920
Farrell and the makers of DS9 faced a lot&nbsp;
of backlash for the kiss. In the Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.440
Deep Space Nine Companion book, producer Ronald D.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.880
Moore said one of their southern affiliates took&nbsp;
the show off the air while another producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.640
Steve Oster, said he received plenty&nbsp;
of calls complaining about the episode.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.240
In the 2018 documentary What We Left Behind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.800
Farrell seems more than content with&nbsp;
her part in the episode, saying:

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.400
"Still to this day, people come&nbsp;
up to me and say, 'Thank you.'"

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:46.320
Sadie Sink entered the cast of Stranger&nbsp;
Things in the show's second season as Max,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.440
eventually becoming part of the team of young&nbsp;
heroes investigating all the crazy stuff going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.480
on in Hawkins, Indiana. While Dustin holds&nbsp;
a torch for Max for most of the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.600
it's Lucas who shares a first kiss with her&nbsp;
in that season's finale. On the face of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.440
there didn't appear to be anything particularly&nbsp;
controversial about the kiss, but that changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.040
once enough fans caught an episode of the&nbsp;
aftershow series, Beyond Stranger Things.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.320
There, Sink revealed her kiss with Lucas wasn't&nbsp;
originally in the script, and she didn't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.640
about it until the day the scene was filmed.&nbsp;
Ross Duffer, one of Stranger Things' showrunners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.600
apparently jokingly told Sink that it was&nbsp;
her fault the kiss was added. Duffer had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.440
previously chided Sink about having her kiss&nbsp;
Lucas, and according to Sink, Duffer said the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.880
fact that she was, quote, "so freaked out" by&nbsp;
the idea is what inspired him to add the scene.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.200
"You reacted so strongly to this. I was just&nbsp;
joking. You were so freaked out that I was like,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.720
"Well, I gotta make her do it now!"

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.840
A lot of fans expressed rage over the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.600
due in no small part to it coming to light&nbsp;
around the same time as the #MeToo movement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.320
In the wake of the controversy, Sink&nbsp;
backtracked a bit, telling TheWrap:

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:08.000
"I mean, of course I was nervous [...] But I never&nbsp;
objected to [it] or felt pushed into anything."

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:15.040
Billy Bob Thornton and Aneglina Jolie met in&nbsp;
1999 on the set of the dramedy Pushing Tin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.880
In spite of a 20-year age difference, they&nbsp;
married in 2000. However, their marriage ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.560
with divorce in 2003. According to Thornton,&nbsp;
the main reason they ultimately didn't work as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:33.600
a couple was their different lifestyles.&nbsp;
In 2018, he told HFPA in Conversation:

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.640
"Hers is a global lifestyle, and&nbsp;
mine is an agoraphobic lifestyle. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.720
that's really — that's the only reason&nbsp;
we're probably not still together."

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.200
Besides this, Thornton said one of the&nbsp;
more memorable stresses on the marriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:53.120
was his explicit scene with Halle Berry&nbsp;
in the 2001 romantic drama Monster's Ball.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.320
Thornton explained to Speaking to The Sun&nbsp;
how filming Monster's Ball made things tough:

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.200
"She is one of the most beautiful women in the&nbsp;
world, and I am talking on the phone to my wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.080
and she says, 'What have you been doing today?'&nbsp;
[...] Other people's situations are hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.400
with areas of doubt. But if you are a&nbsp;
thousand miles from home on a film set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.000
simulating sex with a beautiful&nbsp;
woman, it's even tougher."

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:23.040
Decades after its 1971 release, the dark&nbsp;
comedy Harold and Maude is considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.200
a classic. But the film wasn't so&nbsp;
well-regarded when it hit theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.720
and it helped steer the career trajectory&nbsp;
of its male lead to unwanted destinations.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.240
Harold and Maude is about a death-obsessed&nbsp;
man in his early 20s who drives a hearse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.520
stages bizarre fake suicides for fun,&nbsp;
and regularly attends the funerals of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.360
people he's never met. It's at one of these&nbsp;
funerals that Harold meets the elderly Maude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.800
who likewise goes to funeral services as&nbsp;
a hobby. The two become lovers — a notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.840
that didn't sit well with audiences. The&nbsp;
Guardian reported that Charles Mulvehill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:06.240
one of the film's producers, told Peter Biskind&nbsp;
that no one wanted to see these characters kiss:

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.880
"You couldn't drag people in. The&nbsp;
idea of a 20-year-old boy with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.640
80-year-old woman just made people want to vomit."

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.720
Bud Cort said kissing Ruth Gordon&nbsp;
on the screen was both, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.320
"a blessing and a curse." Professionally&nbsp;
speaking, he was typecast right away and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.840
only offered what he called "weirdo" roles.&nbsp;
He turned them all down, including the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.840
of Billy Bibbit — ultimately played by Brad&nbsp;
Dourif — in One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.280
On the other hand, Cort expressed pride in&nbsp;
helping bring Harold and Maude to life. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.320
told The Guardian he made lasting friendships&nbsp;
on the set of the film, including with Gordon.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.560
In How I Met Your Mother, Jason&nbsp;
Segel got in trouble for kissing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.440
co-star Alyson Hannigan — who played&nbsp;
his wife, Lily — for two reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.600
First, because kissing him bothered Hannigan,&nbsp;
and second, because it put him in debt to her.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.240
Segel was a smoker when the show began production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:10.160
and Hannigan isn't a fan of kissing&nbsp;
smokers. She told Digital Spy in 2008:

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.400
"I cannot stand cigarette smoke."

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.480
She said kissing Segel was, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:20.720
"like kissing an ashtray", clarifying that he&nbsp;
did use things such as mints to mask the taste.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.560
Hannigan explained that Segel tried to use&nbsp;
her distaste of smoking as motivation to quit:

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.520
"When we started the pilot [for How I Met Your&nbsp;
Mother], he was like, 'Get me to stop smoking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.760
I'll be your best friend.' So we did&nbsp;
this bet where he would owe me $10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.120
every time he had a cigarette. After&nbsp;
the first day, he owed me $200."

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.720
Hannigan recalls that Segel succeeded&nbsp;
in giving up cigarettes for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.000
but that he started smoking&nbsp;
again when under stress.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.960
Thankfully, it doesn't seem like the butts&nbsp;
stopped the pair from remaining friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.680
When they appeared together on the The Talk during&nbsp;
the lead up to the How I Met Your Mother finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.240
Hannigan openly wept at the idea&nbsp;
of not working with Segel anymore.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.320
Tommy Davidson claimed in his 2020 memoir&nbsp;
Living in Color: What's Funny About Me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:15.760
that kissing Jada Pinkett Smith while&nbsp;
filming the 1999 romantic comedy Woo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.920
almost got him into a fistfight&nbsp;
with her husband, Will Smith.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:25.360
According to his memoir, neither he nor his&nbsp;
co-star were happy with how the film ended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.800
So, Davidson said he pitched the idea that&nbsp;
actually wound up being the film's finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.440
with him and Jada Pinkett Smith dancing&nbsp;
in the middle of a city street. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:40.400
Davidson and Smith had agreed on the new&nbsp;
ending, Davidson added something without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.840
letting Jada know first. When they filmed the&nbsp;
scene, Davidson kissed her without warning.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.800
Davidson says as soon as&nbsp;
the scene was done filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.880
an enraged Will Smith followed him into his&nbsp;
trailer, where things nearly got violent:

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.640
"If a black man get mad at you from Philly,&nbsp;
and come at you, you best be ready."

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:03.920
Thankfully for everyone involved, the actress&nbsp;
stepped in and calmed things down between her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.960
husband and her co-star. Davidson says that&nbsp;
he and Smith remain good friends to this day.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.440
William Shatner doesn't enjoy the best reputation&nbsp;
in terms of how he treats his co-stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:20.880
but there's at least one scene in Star Trek: The&nbsp;
Original Series that Shatner, Nichelle Nichols,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:26.480
and anyone else involved should be proud of,&nbsp;
no matter what former colleagues say of them.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:32.320
In the 1968 Star Trek episode "Plato's&nbsp;
Stepchildren" — airing only a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:37.760
after the U.S. Supreme Court deemed interracial&nbsp;
marriage legal — Shatner and Nichols treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:43.760
audiences to the first kiss between black and&nbsp;
white actors on scripted TV. In the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.000
Shatner's Captain Kirk and Nichols'&nbsp;
Lt. Uhura are forced to kiss each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.640
other by a group of aliens who style&nbsp;
themselves after the ancient Greeks.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:58.640
In her 1994 memoir, Beyond Uhura, Nichols wrote&nbsp;
that NBC wasn't happy when they learned of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.760
kissing scene, and a deal was struck between the&nbsp;
network and Star Trek's producers that two scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:10.000
would be filmed — one in which Kirk and Uhura&nbsp;
kiss and one in which they didn't. Not wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.840
the kiss-less scene to air, Shatner and Nichols&nbsp;
purposely bungled them so they wouldn't be usable.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:21.440
While NBC was worried the episode wouldn't&nbsp;
play well in the South, Nichols, Shatner and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.720
the rest of the cast and crew were disappointed&nbsp;
by a very different locale's reaction. "Plato's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:33.200
Stepchildren" joined a list of four Star Trek: The&nbsp;
Original Series episodes the BBC refused to air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.720
until 1994. The British broadcaster's official&nbsp;
reason for banning the episode, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.440
was the sadism displayed by the episode's&nbsp;
villains rather than the interracial kiss.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:50.800
In season 3 of the crime anthology series Fargo,&nbsp;
Ewan McGregor pulls double duty as twin brothers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:57.120
Emmit and Ray Stussy. Emmit is a successful&nbsp;
businessman, and Ray is a down-on-his-luck parole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.960
officer. The conflict between the brothers gets&nbsp;
heated after Ray falls for one of his parolees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:08.240
Nikki -- played by Mary Elizabeth Winstead.&nbsp;
She convinces Ray that his brother owes him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:13.120
more than he's given. At first, you might&nbsp;
get the impression Nikki is using Ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.920
for her own benefit, but it soon becomes&nbsp;
clear her feelings for him are genuine.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:23.440
It apparently wasn't too difficult for Winstead to&nbsp;
act like she loved Ray Stussy because it wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.560
be too long before McGregor and Winstead&nbsp;
would become a couple -- in spite of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.640
being married when they met on the set. While&nbsp;
we don't know exactly when their relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:41.280
started, it seems clear that working together on&nbsp;
Fargo had something to do with it. In May 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:48.000
a month after Fargo's season 3 finale, Winstead&nbsp;
announced her break-up with husband Riley Stearns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:54.080
And early in 2018, McGregor filed for&nbsp;
divorce from his wife of 22 years.&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:58.960
After McGregor and Winstead were spotted&nbsp;
kissing in public, People claimed that an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.680
unnamed source said the pair had been&nbsp;
involved since Winstead's separation.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:10.080
In November 2017, Star Trek: Discovery&nbsp;
featured the first kiss between two men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.880
in the franchise. In the episode "Into&nbsp;
the Forest I Go," Paul Stamets nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:21.440
dies when he uses his body to perform 133&nbsp;
consecutive warps with Discovery's spore drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:26.400
in order to give the crew the edge they need&nbsp;
over the Klingon's ship, the Sarcophagus.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.680
"If we can save Pahvo, defeat the Klingons&nbsp;
and do all this, 133 jumps it is."

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:37.920
After the battle is won, Stamets agrees&nbsp;
to one more jump to a Federation starbase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.960
On his way to make the jump, he finds his&nbsp;
husband, Dr. Hugh Culber, and gives him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.480
a loving kiss before assuring him that after&nbsp;
he's done, they'll be taking a long vacation.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.560
Actors Wilson Cruz and Anthony Rapp predictably&nbsp;
faced a backlash from part of the audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.760
In an eloquent and cutting Facebook post, Cruz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:02.240
let those fans know he wasn't going&nbsp;
to be made to feel guilty for a kiss:

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.760
"I'm not here for your comfort.&nbsp;
That's not why we are here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.080
We're here to grow. Star Trek is and&nbsp;
has always been here to challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:15.920
you to look outside of yourself and to see&nbsp;
other people and other experiences in yourself."

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.360
In at least one case, it wasn't&nbsp;
so much an onscreen kiss that got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:26.560
an actor in trouble as much as it was an&nbsp;
avoided kiss that helped to tank a movie.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:32.960
In spite of superstars Johnny Depp and Angelina&nbsp;
Jolie in the lead roles for the 2010 romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:38.320
thriller The Tourist, critics panned the&nbsp;
flick. One of the more recurring criticisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:42.800
leveled against the film is the lack of&nbsp;
chemistry between its romantic leads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:47.680
including kissing scenes that failed&nbsp;
to excite anyone. A story that leaked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:53.440
about a month before The Tourist's release&nbsp;
may provide a hint as to what went wrong.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:58.880
In November 2010, the Daily Mail reported&nbsp;
that audiences would be cheated out of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.600
steamy shower scene between Depp and&nbsp;
Jolie. According to their source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:08.480
when Depp's longtime girlfriend, Vanessa&nbsp;
Paradis, learned of the sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:14.240
she reportedly begged Depp to pull out of&nbsp;
the film. Depp refused to quit the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:19.920
but he gave Paradis the concession that he&nbsp;
would demand the shower scene be scrapped.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:26.320
It's only speculation, but considering what Depp&nbsp;
was willing to do to placate Paradis' jealousy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:29.680
it doesn't feel like a stretch&nbsp;
to wonder whether or not this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:32.400
impacted his overall performance in the film.

